# Internationalisation

Supported locales in v1: **`en`** (source) and **`fr`**. Every user-facing UI string change must update **both** catalogs in the same change — never ship an English-only key.

## Where strings go

| Surface | Mechanism | Catalogs |
|---------|-----------|----------|
| `apps/vscode` host | `vscode.l10n.t` / `package.nls` | `package.nls.json` + `package.nls.fr.json`, `l10n/bundle.l10n*.json` |
| `apps/vscode` webview | `@vscode/l10n` | webview locale bundles |
| `apps/cli`, `apps/acp-agent` | local `t()` helper | `locales/en.json` + `fr.json` |

## Rules

- No hardcoded user-facing copy in templates, TSX, or TS for UI chrome (labels, buttons, nav, form errors, empty states, tooltips, aria-labels that users hear).
- Add the key to **en** and **fr** together. Key names are stable English dotted ids (`nav.signIn`, `auth.login.title`).
- Use interpolation (`{name}` / l10n args), never concatenate translated fragments into a sentence.
- Language selection: auto (browser / VS Code display language / `LANG`) with a persisted manual override. Fallback: `en`.

## Out of scope (do not force through UI i18n)

- Model-facing text in `packages/core` / `packages/tools` (system prompts, tool descriptions) — keep English for the model.
- Internal logs, telemetry event names, and developer-only stderr that is not product UI.
